function diagram(){let c=document.createElement('canvas');c.width=1100;c.height=380;let x=c.getContext('2d');x.fillStyle='#f6faf6';x.fillRect(0,0,1100,380);x.font='bold 24px Arial';x.fillStyle='#153e39';x.fillText('ILLUSTRATIVE SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE',40,48);['Student browser','Application logic','Library database'].forEach((t,i)=>{let left=40+i*355;x.fillStyle='#e0ede4';x.fillRect(left,120,310,130);x.strokeStyle='#6e9e83';x.strokeRect(left,120,310,130);x.fillStyle='#153e39';x.textAlign='center';x.font='bold 23px Arial';x.fillText(t,left+155,191);if(i<2){x.beginPath();x.moveTo(left+315,185);x.lineTo(left+344,185);x.stroke();x.fillText('›',left+339,192)}});x.textAlign='left';x.font='18px Arial';x.fillStyle='#64756c';x.fillText('Demo diagram only. Replace with your own design.',40,328);return{data:c.toDataURL('image/png'),name:'system-architecture.png',caption:'Illustrative library system architecture (demo)',w:1100,h:380,anchor:'start',automatic:true}}
function sample(){if($('content').value.trim()&&!confirm('Replace this document with the demo? Save a backup first if you want to keep your work.'))return;chooseType('blackbook');Object.entries({title:'Smart campus library',student:'Demo Student',roll:'DEMO-001',college:'Sample College of Technology',course:'BCA · Computer science',year:'2026–27',guide:'Demo Faculty',font:'serif',layout:'chapters',university:'Sample University',degree:'Bachelor of Computer Applications',students:'Demo Student | DEMO-001',institutionDetails:'Department of Computer Applications',reportDate:'',recommendation:'',certificateText:'',acknowledgement:'This demonstration shows an acknowledgement page. Replace this text with your own thanks and describe only help you actually received.'}).forEach(([k,v])=>$(k).value=v);$('certificate').checked=true;$('content').value=`Abstract\n\nThis sample describes a proposed campus library system. It demonstrates document structure and formatting only. It does not report an actual deployment, measured results or an issued certificate.\n\nIntroduction\n\n1.1 Project overview\n\nA campus library manages books, member records and issue history. When these records are kept in separate files, staff may need to repeat data entry and students may not know whether a book is available. The proposed system brings the main records into one place.\n\nThe project focuses on a small college library. Its purpose is to make catalogue search and issue tracking easier while keeping member information available only to authorised staff.\n\nObjectives\n\n- Provide a searchable book catalogue.\n- Keep issue and return records consistent.\n- Restrict changes to authorised staff.\n- Give users a clear view of book availability.\n\nExisting system\n\nIn the example workflow, staff use a register to record book issues. A student asks the librarian about availability, and the librarian checks the register and shelf. This is an illustrative scenario, not a surveyed claim about any real institution.\n\nProposed system\n\nThe proposed web application lets a student search by book title or author. A librarian can add books, issue available copies, and record returns. Each change is associated with a staff account so that records can be checked later.\n\nSystem design\n\nThe architecture separates the user interface, application logic and data storage. The browser displays the catalogue. The application checks permissions and updates records. The database stores books, members and transactions. The attached diagram is an illustration of this design.\n\nBook records would contain a title, author, identifier and available copy count. An issue record would connect a member to a copy and store the issue and due dates. Input validation would prevent missing identifiers and invalid dates.\n\nImplementation\n\nA prototype could start with the catalogue and staff login. Issue and return functions would be added after the basic data model is tested. Secrets should not be included in source code. Member details should be collected only when needed and protected from unauthorised access.\n\nThe interface should support a phone as well as a desktop computer. Labels should be readable, controls should have clear names, and error messages should tell the user how to correct an input.\n\nTesting\n\nThe following are planned test cases, not completed test results:\n\n- Search for an existing title and an unknown title.\n- Try issuing a book with no available copies.\n- Confirm that returning a book changes its availability.\n- Attempt a staff-only action without permission.\n- Check the interface at a phone width.\n\nResults and discussion\n\nNo production results are claimed in this sample. In a real project, this section should contain observed outcomes, screenshots from the working application and the method used to measure improvements. Both successful and unsuccessful test cases should be reported.\n\nConclusion\n\nThe proposed system is intended to bring library records into one consistent workflow. The next step would be to implement a prototype, test it with realistic sample records and review it with library staff.\n\nFuture scope\n\nLater work could include reminders, barcode lookup and accessibility testing. These are possible improvements, not features already delivered by this sample project.\n\nReferences\n\nThis demonstration has no research sources. Replace this note with the actual books, papers, websites and software documentation used in your project. Do not submit the sample as original research.`;images=[diagram()];sections=[];organise();generate()}
$('sample').onclick=sample;$('sample2').onclick=sample;


